 I conducted a complete, read-only inspection of your entire project without altering any of your code or configuration.
  A detailed, line-by-line audit report has been compiled and saved to the artifact:
  👉 **`codebase_audit_report.md`**
  ──────
  ### Overall Opinion & Architectural Assessment
  • Architecture & Structure (8.5/10):
  The codebase follows clean layered architecture principles very well: clear domain separation (auth, user, customer-address, restaurant), centralized DI via tsyringe, strong validation with class-validator,
  structured error handling with AppError, and proper database transaction management.
  • Current Operational State (Critical Issues):
  Despite the strong architectural foundation, the API currently cannot run in production or pass end-to-end tests due to a set of runtime blockers, broken registration logic, and SQL syntax errors in the
  migrations.
  ──────
  ### High-Level Summary of Findings

  #### 1. Critical Runtime Blockers (Server Freezes / Crashes)

  • All HTTP Requests Hang: In correlationID.ts:4-8, the middleware never calls next(). Every request entering the server times out.
  • All Controller Actions Throw TypeError: Route callbacks (e.g. userRouter.get('/:id', userController.getProfile)) lose their this context in Express. Accessing this.userService or this.authService crashes
  with Cannot read properties of undefined.
  • Registration is Blocked: In UserService.existsByEmail, it throws UserNotFoundError when a user does not exist. Calling userService.create() triggers this check, meaning no new user can ever register.
  • Password Reset Expiration Inverted: In PasswordResetEntity.isExpired, it checks this.expiresAt > new Date(). Valid OTPs are rejected as expired, while truly expired OTPs pass.
  • Health Check Hangs: health.route.ts:5-11 awaits the database ping but never sends a response (res.json()).

  #### 2. Database Migrations (All 4 Migrations Fail)

  • create_users_table.ts: Invalid SQL syntax NOT NULL IN CHECK(...) and missing semicolon before CREATE INDEX.
  • create_password_resets.ts: Unclosed parenthesis in foreign key REFERENCES users(id,.
  • create_customer_addresses.ts: Broken index syntax CREATE INDEX ... ON (users); instead of ON customer_addresses(user_id).
  • create_restaurants_table.ts: Typo 'penging' instead of 'pending' in check constraint, and column misspelled as ceated_at (breaking the created_at index).

  #### 3. Data Integrity & Repository Bugs

  • Inverted Conditions: In CustomerAddressRepository.update, lat, street, and building use === undefined, wiping them when not supplied and ignoring them when provided.
  • Missing Record Crashes: findById in CustomerAddressRepository and finById in RestaurantRepository unconditionally pass undefined to toEntity(), crashing with a TypeError instead of returning undefined.
  • Field Mapping Discrepancy: In RestaurantRepository.toEntity, row.createdAt and row.updatedAt are used instead of snake_case row.created_at and row.updated_at.
  • Phone Not Updating: In UserRepository.update, data.phone is ignored, and created_at is overwritten instead of updated_at.

  #### 4. Security & Access Control

  • IDOR (Missing Ownership Checks): Any authenticated user can view or edit any profile via PATCH /api/user/:id, or manage any address in /api/address/:id.
  • Missing Rate Limiting & Security Headers: Auth endpoints (/login, /forget-password, /reset-password) lack rate limiting (brute-force risk on 6-digit OTPs), and helmet / cors middlewares are missing.
  • Auth Error Handling: If an access token is expired or forged, authenticate throws an unhandled JWT error that surfaces as a 500 Internal Server Error rather than 401 Unauthorized.
  ──────
  ### Key Decisions / Next Steps

  When you're ready, we can address these fixes together:

  1. Phase 1 (Blockers): Fix next() in correlationID.ts, bind controller methods, and fix userService.existsByEmail.
  2. Phase 2 (Database): Correct the 4 migration scripts and run them cleanly against PostgreSQL.
  3. Phase 3 (Repositories & Security): Fix repository mapping/conditions and enforce ownership checks (req.user.userId === targetId).

  Have a good rest, and let me know how you'd like to proceed when you return!
                                                                                                   